00:51:27.900 --> 00:51:31.020
<v Miki Meek>But how nervous are you? I mean, literally, now you're face to face.
<v Miki Meek>但你有多紧张？我的意思是，实际上，现在你们面对面了。

00:51:31.020 --> 00:51:40.500
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I'm shaking. I'm very nervous, definitely. And I think there's a part of me that is still thinking, I could just talk to her right now. Erin. Hey, Erin. I could just say that.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我在发抖。我很紧张，当然。我认为我内心深处有一部分仍然在想，我现在就可以和她交谈。艾琳。嘿，艾琳。我可以那样说。

00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:53.160
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I could hear myself saying, Erin Hart, I know you probably don't recognize me. I know you don't know me. But I am Laurel Jessamyn Lovell, and you used my ID to do a number of things.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我能听到自己说，艾琳·哈特，我知道你可能不认识我。我知道你不认识我。但我叫劳雷尔·杰萨敏·洛弗尔，你用我的证件做了很多事情。

00:51:53.160 --> 00:52:11.700
<v Jessamyn Lovell>But the power for me was in seeing her without her knowing that I was seeing her. And then she disappears into the dressing room. And that's when the other PI comes over to me and says, she's stealing clothes. And I was like, what?
<v Jessamyn Lovell>但对我来说，力量在于看着她而她不知道我在看她。然后她消失在试衣间里。那是另一个私人调查员走过来对我说，她在偷衣服。我说，什么？

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:24.960
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I just needed to get out of there. I was overwhelmed. I was feeling really sad for her. And I didn't want to feel sad for her. I wanted to feel angry at her.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我只需要离开那里。我不知所措。我为她感到难过。我不想为她感到难过。我想对她生气。

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:32.470
<v Miki Meek>It was hard to maintain the anger, because she was looking at Erin and remembering things she'd done when she was young and her family was broke.
<v Miki Meek>很难保持愤怒，因为她在看着艾琳，回忆起她年轻且家庭破产时做过的事情。

00:52:32.470 --> 00:52:52.140
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I had shoplifted, like, nice cheese. Or like, I literally stole arugula. Like, things that I thought and associated with class, middle to upper middle class. And I think that's what really was giving me this sympathy for Erin Hart was I could identify, in a way, with the desire to be someone else.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我偷过像好的奶酪之类的东西。或者像，我真的偷了芝麻菜。就像，我关联到阶级，中产阶级到中上阶级的东西。我想这正是真正让我对艾琳·哈特产生同情心的原因，因为我可以从某种程度上认同想成为别人的欲望。

00:52:52.140 --> 00:53:08.610
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I could see how a path in my life could have led to my being in that moment. I really could. But it was, I guess, a little disappointing. I guess it was a little like, oh, I kind of wanted it to be like she was a criminal and did something bad to me.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我能看到我生命中的一条道路是如何导致我在那一刻出现在那里的。我真的能。但我想，这有点令人失望。我想有点像，哦，我本来希望她是个罪犯，对我做了什么坏事。

00:53:08.610 --> 00:53:08.910
<v Miki Meek>Yeah.
<v Miki Meek>是的。

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:31.320
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And it just wasn't that. None of that satisfaction of "I got her." It was just this weird transition. And anger was starting to be sort of replaced with empathy.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>而事实并非如此。那种“我抓到她了”的满足感一点都没有。这只是那种奇怪的转变。愤怒开始被同情所取代。

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:51.000
<v Miki Meek>What Jessamyn did next doesn't sound empathetic at all when you first hear about it. But for Jessamyn, it was the logical end of research. She went through the hundreds of images she'd taken, and she put together a gallery exhibit. It had surveillance shots of Erin, the court summons, police reports, bills, and pictures of places where her ID had been used.
<v Miki Meek>杰萨敏接下来做的事情当你第一次听到时一点也不让人同情。但对杰萨敏来说，这是研究的逻辑终点。她翻看了她拍的数百张照片，并将它们整理成一个画廊展览。里面有艾琳的监控截图、法庭传票、警方报告、账单以及她的证件被使用的地点的图片。

00:53:51.000 --> 00:54:05.350
<v Miki Meek>She'd done this once before with the surveillance photos she took of her dad. In fact, her wallet was stolen while she was setting up that exhibit, which is what sent her down the rabbit hole of trying to find Erin in the first place. She called the new show, "Dear Erin Hart."
<v Miki Meek>她在拍摄她爸爸的监控照片时做过一次。事实上，她的钱包是在她筹备那个展览时被偷的，这正是她陷入追踪艾琳的兔洞的起因。她把新展览命名为“亲爱的艾琳·哈特”。

00:54:05.350 --> 00:54:19.310
<v Miki Meek>And one of the items on display was a sealed letter in a Plexiglas box. She asked the people at the gallery to give it to Erin if she ever showed up there. It wasn't a dramatic letter, just a way of telling Erin that she'd been looking for her and wanted to talk.
<v Miki Meek>展品中有一封放在有机玻璃盒里的密封信。她要求画廊的人如果艾琳出现的话就把信交给她。那不是一封戏剧性的信，只是一种告诉艾琳她在找她并想谈谈的方式。

00:54:19.310 --> 00:54:24.830
<v Miki Meek>Jessamyn didn't see putting Erin's name and photos out in public as revenge. But not everyone saw it that way.
<v Miki Meek>杰萨敏并不认为把艾琳的名字和照片公开是一种报复。但并不是所有人都这么看。

00:54:24.830 --> 00:54:30.170
<v News Reporter>If you were a victim of identity theft, would you consider tracking down the thief and turning the tables?
<v News Reporter>如果你是身份盗窃的受害者，你会考虑追踪小偷并反击吗？

00:54:30.170 --> 00:54:31.430
<v Miki Meek>This is from the Today Show.
<v Miki Meek>这是来自《今日秀》。

00:54:31.430 --> 00:54:38.780
<v News Reporter>In an act some have described as revenge, Lovell followed and photographed the woman who stole her wallet, and then her identity.
<v News Reporter>在一些人描述为复仇的行为中，洛弗尔追踪并拍摄了那个偷走她钱包，然后偷走她身份的人。

00:54:38.780 --> 00:55:05.670
<v Miki Meek>I reached out to Erin Hart through her probation officer to talk about all this. I sent a couple of emails listing everything Jessamyn says about her. But Erin never got back to me. So I couldn't verify whether she was responsible for bills Jessamyn got from Hertz and Whole Foods, or any of the unpaid parking tickets and tolls, or whether she shoplifted from Goodwill or stole from her boyfriend.
<v Miki Meek>我通过艾琳·哈特的缓刑官联系了她谈论这一切。我发了几封电子邮件，列出了杰萨敏关于她的一切说法。但艾琳从没回复我。所以我无法核实她是否要为杰萨敏收到的来自赫兹和全食超市的账单，或任何未付的停车罚单和通行费负责，或者她是否从好运来偷窃，或者偷了她男朋友的东西。

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:23.860
<v Miki Meek>So Erin secretly posed as Jessamyn, and Jessamyn secretly photographed Erin. Jessamyn admits it's an uncomfortable thing that they have in common. Each of them is willing to invade someone else's privacy to get what they want.
<v Miki Meek>所以艾琳秘密地冒充杰萨敏，而杰萨敏秘密地拍摄了艾琳。杰萨敏承认这是一件不舒服的事情，也是她们共同点。她们每个人都愿意侵犯别人的隐私以得到她们想要的东西。

00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:43.970
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is one of the producers of our show. Like I said earlier, today's show was first broadcast a couple years ago. Since then, Jessamyn has become a licensed private investigator. She's taken courses in self-defense, weapons training, research. She says her skills are in high demand.
<v Ira Glass>米基·米克是我们节目的制片人之一。像我之前说过的，今天的节目是几年前第一次广播的。从那时起，杰萨敏成了一名持证私人调查员。她选修了自卫、武器训练、研究课程。她说她的技能需求量很大。

00:55:43.970 --> 00:55:56.490
<v Ira Glass>We thought we would end today's program on an airplane. At some point in putting together this week's show, it occurred to us that an overnight flight really is just like this huge bed, with hundreds of people all together, dreaming different dreams.
<v Ira Glass>我们想以在飞机上结束今天的节目。在准备这一周节目的某个时候，我们想到一次过夜飞行确实就像这张巨大的床，有数百人在一起，梦想着不同的梦想。

00:55:56.490 --> 00:56:08.130
<v Ira Glass>And we asked people on two different flights like this to tell us their dreams, and their daydreams, and their hopes and dreams. One of our staff was actually on a red eye flight herself and got on the plane's PA system to explain the premise.
<v Ira Glass>我们要求人们在两架这样的飞机上告诉我们他们的梦想、他们的白日梦，以及他们的希望和梦想。我们的一名员工实际上就在一架红眼航班上，并使用了飞机的PA系统来解释这个前提。

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:12.990
<v Stephanie Foo>May I have your attention, please, ladies and gentlemen? My name is Stephanie Foo.
<v Stephanie Foo>请注意，女士们先生们。我的名字是斯蒂芬妮·富。

00:56:12.990 --> 00:56:16.400
<v Ira Glass>Somehow, being on the PA system, she started talking like a flight attendant.
<v Ira Glass>不知怎么的，在使用PA系统时，她开始像空乘人员那样说话。

00:56:16.400 --> 00:56:20.010
<v Stephanie Foo>I am doing a story about this flight.
<v Stephanie Foo>我正在做一个关于这个航班的故事。

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:24.450
<v Ira Glass>David Weinberg and Jessica Ament recorded people as well. Here are some of them.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·温伯格和杰西卡·阿门也录制了人们的声音。这里是其中的一些。

00:56:24.450 --> 00:56:35.820
<v Man>I was dreaming about old fashioned TV antennas. And then at one point, I felt like I was shaking. And I'm sure it was because the plane was shaking. But I was trying to adjust the antenna.
<v Man>我梦见老式电视天线。然后在某个时刻，我觉得我在颤抖。我敢肯定那是因为飞机在颤抖。但我试着调整天线。

00:56:35.820 --> 00:56:43.830
<v Woman>I was dreaming about-- I was in a hot air balloon. And I was yelling for my dog. "Hey, Katie, I'm up here."
<v Woman>我梦见——我坐在热气球里。我正在大喊我的狗。“嘿，凯蒂，我在上面。”

00:56:43.830 --> 00:57:01.210
<v Man>Sitting there, semi-lucid, I was thinking of the horned puffins swimming underwater, and how the little bubbles come out of their feathers when they're swimming. And then I thought of them as going along in space and creating stars, and then diving into the center of the galaxy.
<v Man>坐在那里，半清醒，我想着角海雀在水下游泳，以及当它们游泳时小气泡是如何从它们的羽毛中冒出来的。然后我想到它们在太空中穿行，创造恒星，然后潜入星系中心。

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:02.580
<v Jessica Ament>That's really trippy.
<v Jessica Ament>那真的很迷幻。

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:03.490
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>是啊。

00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:04.950
<v Jessica Ament>Were you medicated at all?
<v Jessica Ament>你服药了吗？

00:57:04.950 --> 00:57:12.670
<v Man>No, not at all. [LAUGHS]
<v Man>没有，完全没有。[笑]

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:32.530
<v Woman>I had a total nightmare. I was backing down a hill out of a garage with a friend in a VW bus. And we lost control of it and went down the hill. And my friend turned to me and said, you know, I will never forgive you for this.
<v Woman>我做了一个噩梦。我和一个朋友坐在一辆大众巴士里倒下一座山，出了车库。我们失去了控制，冲下了山。我朋友转过来对我说，你知道，我永远不会原谅你。

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:52.040
<v Woman>Everybody says, why would you want to go to New York? Ew, it's so big. And why? And I'm like, because you see all these things on television. I have always wanted to experience a cab ride in New York City. We're going to do it all. We're going to do it all.
<v Woman>每个人都说，你为什么要来纽约？呃，它太大了。为什么？我就像，因为你在电视上看到了所有这些东西。我一直想体验纽约市的出租车旅行。我们要做完这一切。我们要做完这一切。

00:57:52.040 --> 00:58:26.450
<v Man>My daughter's about to have a baby. So I don't know if dream's exactly the right word, but a lot of hopes. It's her first baby, my first grandbaby. Just to put it simply, I definitely didn't live up to my responsibility as her father. And I had not been a regular part of her life up until she was nine. I blew it. So I'm definitely not going to make the same mistakes that I made with her. I feel like I've been given a second chance.
<v Man>我女儿快要生孩子了。所以我不知道梦想是否是准确的词，但有很多希望。这是她的第一个孩子，我的第一个孙子。简单地说，我绝对没有履行我作为父亲的责任。在她9岁之前，我没有定期出现在她的生活中。我搞砸了。所以我绝对不会犯我以前犯过的同样的错误。我觉得我得到了第二次机会。

00:58:26.450 --> 00:58:44.970
<v Man>[MUSIC - "SAME BED, DIFFERENT DREAMS," BY JOHN HOWARD]
<v Man>[音乐 - “同床异梦”，由约翰·霍华德演唱]

00:58:44.970 --> 00:58:54.460
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Zoe Chace and me with Sean Cole, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Joe Richman, Robyn Semien Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由佐伊·蔡斯和我制作，还有肖恩·科尔、斯蒂芬妮·富、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、布莱恩·里德、乔·里奇曼、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克。

00:58:54.460 --> 00:59:03.280
<v Ira Glass>Senior producer for today's program is Julie Snyder. Editing help from Joel Lovell. Research help from Michelle Harris and Christopher Swetala. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的资深制片人是朱莉·斯奈德。编辑帮助来自乔尔·洛弗尔。研究帮助来自米歇尔·哈里斯和克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉。音乐帮助来自达米安·格拉夫和罗伯·格迪斯。

00:59:03.280 --> 00:59:18.850
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Clinton Cargil, Sam Greenspan, Sarah Stieger, Ian Shapira, Alaska Airlines, Gonzalo Martinez de Vedia, and The Worker Justice Center of New York, Antonio Cericido of Latino USA, Jeff Singer, Chris Crawford, and Cece Chen.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢克林顿·卡吉尔、萨姆·格林斯潘、莎拉·斯蒂格、伊恩·夏皮拉、阿拉斯加航空公司、贡萨洛·马丁内斯·德·维迪亚和纽约工人司法中心、Latino USA的安东尼奥·塞里西多、杰夫·辛格、克里斯·克劳福德和塞西·陈。

00:59:18.850 --> 00:59:27.280
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播交易所PRX传送给公共广播电台。

00:59:27.280 --> 00:59:34.960
<v Ira Glass>Thanks, as always, to our programs co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia, who, of course, stopped by the office. He's wearing the same outfit today that he wears every single day.
<v Ira Glass>像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生，他当然顺便来办公室看了看。他今天穿的衣服和他每天穿的一模一样。

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:38.240
<v Miki Meek>Tight black shirt, shiny pants, and wedge boots.
<v Miki Meek>紧身黑衬衫、亮面裤子和坡跟靴。

00:59:38.240 --> 00:59:41.920
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of this American life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带更多关于这个美国生活的故事回来。

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:43.420
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "ONLY IN MY DREAMS," BY DEBBIE GIBSON]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “只在我的梦中”，由黛比·吉布森演唱]

00:59:43.420 --> 00:59:48.420
<v Ira Glass>(SINGING) Don't turn the record over. Drop the needle at the start. And put your hand back on my shoulder. I'm dreaming, dreaming away the time.
<v Ira Glass>（歌唱）不要翻唱片。从头放下针。把你的手放回我的肩膀上。我在梦想，梦想着消磨时光。
